course: dictionary
lesson: 1626
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. Chew is one of them. It can mean breaking food up with your teeth, biting something again and again, or thinking hard about something. The words around it tell you which meaning is right. «Maddie's ball gets chewed to shreds by the neighbour's Staffie» is about a dog biting a ball until it is in pieces, so chew means to bite again and again.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. For food, does it make sense to say «I chew my sandwich»? Yes. For a dog, does it make sense to say «the dog chews its bone»? Yes. Each meaning fits a different sentence, so the context shows you which one to use.
explain: Some phrases keep their own meaning. «Bite off more than you can chew» does not mean eating a huge mouthful. It means taking on too much work. To chew something over means to think about it carefully, so «Mum chewed over the plan» means she thought about it hard.
fact: To chew is to break up food in your mouth with your teeth before you swallow it.
fact: A chew is a chewy sweet, or a treat for a dog.
fact: Chewing is the first step of digestion: your teeth cut and grind food, and saliva starts to break it down.
fact: Chew food well and chew with your mouth closed. Small children should not have hard, round foods, because they can choke on them.
fact: Chewing gum goes in the bin, not on the floor or under desks.
exercise: A rabbit's teeth grow all its life. Without enough hay to chew, they can grow into sharp spikes. What does chew mean here? | To break up food with the teeth before swallowing | To paint something in bright colours | *To bite and grind food with the teeth | To sleep for a long time
exercise: «Some dogs left too long bark, howl or chew out of worry.» What does chew mean in this sentence? | Swallow something whole | *Bite something again and again | Sleep | Sing loudly
exercise: Dad says, «I need to chew over this plan before I decide.» What does chew over mean? | *Think about it carefully | Tear the plan up | Eat the plan | Shout at the plan
test: Which of these is a chew? | A ball that bounces | A dog's lead | A dog's bed | *A chewy treat for a dog
test: «Bite off more than you can chew» means | To have very sharp teeth | *To take on too much | To eat a very big bite | To chew very slowly
exam: Which step of digestion does chewing start? | Swallowing the food whole | Drinking water after a meal | Sleeping after a meal | *Teeth cutting and grinding the food
